of giants way back when during that move

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.250
from the East Coast over to Texas.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.070
How did Otis and
Rosie make this all happen?

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:18.762
&gt;&gt; Well again,

00:03:18.762 --> 00:03:24.077
it's such a privilege to have the honor
to speak with all of you and you're right

00:03:24.077 --> 00:03:29.690
Adrian when I woke up on that Saturday I
probably did not get to the gym.

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:34.740
But I will say that I woke up and
continued to be full of gratitude.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:39.469
And you can't have the moment
of being a CEO of a company like

00:03:39.469 --> 00:03:42.760
TIAA without being reflected.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:47.750
And that brings me to,your question when
you think about Otis and Rosie Brown.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:51.830
They are my anchor, they are my roots.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:56.609
And when I think about what they mean
to me, my dad taught me to reach for

00:03:56.609 --> 00:04:01.950
the moon, and he would say even if you
miss you would be among the stars.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:05.240
And I am still reaching for that moon.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:09.623
My mom would always tell me,
whatever you do, you know you do,

00:04:09.623 --> 00:04:15.130
you do it with excellence or you don't
do it at all, no matter what it is.

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:19.270
And so those just life lessons along
the way, not just what they said but

00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:20.980
what they embodied.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:25.110
Not what they embody but what I saw
growing up and all the sacrifices.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:30.220
It's all of that, that allows me
to be in the seat that I am today.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:33.689
And again, being on the shoulders
of my parents and so

00:04:33.689 --> 00:04:38.250
many others before me, has me full
of gratitude and a lot of humility.

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:43.968
&gt;&gt; Absolutely and there were ton of
sacrifices your parents right you told

00:04:43.968 --> 00:04:48.758
the story about driving with your car five
of you pack in there every hand in that

00:04:48.758 --> 00:04:50.883
car so that says something right.

00:04:50.883 --> 00:04:53.509
&gt;&gt; Now,
it doesn't mean when you think about it,

00:04:53.509 --> 00:04:56.170
I'm first generation integration.

00:04:56.170 --> 00:05:01.579
And it seems so long ago when we talked
about the course of our society,

00:05:01.579 --> 00:05:04.697
my dad grew up in the segregated South and

00:05:04.697 --> 00:05:08.490
Louisiana my mother grew up,
I'm in Alabama.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:10.382
They met in New York, but

00:05:10.382 --> 00:05:15.260
when we moved to Texas my parents
packed everything that we own.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:18.127
So it was, three children,
my mom and my dad and

00:05:18.127 --> 00:05:23.490
everything that we owned, which is you
can imagine in a car that's not much.

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:28.083
And I always say that that was a real
moment because when I think about it,

00:05:28.083 --> 00:05:31.265
when I started my life in
Texas sitting on crates.

00:05:31.265 --> 00:05:36.032
And when you think about where you've
been, and you think about where you are,

00:05:36.032 --> 00:05:39.090
you can't help but
think through those moments.

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:44.950
And I think it's important that we
are okay talking about those moments.

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:48.600
Because the reality is,
it's those moments that anchor us.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:52.540
It's those moments that teach
us empathy or humility.

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:55.910
Those moments that showed us grit and
tenacity.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:06:01.130
And so we meet and I've met so
many amazing people along the way.

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:05.747
But the real lessons come from those
everyday people and for me, that was Otis

00:06:05.747 --> 00:06:10.434
and Rosie brown when I think about their
sacrifice and trying to do the very best

00:06:10.434 --> 00:06:15.470
that they could to provide for us even in
moments, their best was not sufficient.

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:16.670
It was their best.

00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:18.510
And, I'm forever grateful for that.

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:23.030
&gt;&gt; I'm sure they're
extremely proud of you.

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:25.590
And you said it yourself, right?

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:29.740
You can't forget some of these folks
that are making the sacrifices.

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:32.099
You've said when you
are on the 50th floor at

00:06:32.099 --> 00:06:32.871
JP Morgan-
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:06:32.871 --> 00:06:35.100
&gt;&gt; It's the people that
are in the mail room.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:39.530
It's the reception, the cooks,
that fact, the ceiling, right?

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:40.230
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:42.600
And a lot of times people say,
well, what do you mean?

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.670
And again, being anchored in history.

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:49.234
And when you think about when and
how did my melanin,

00:06:49.234 --> 00:06:52.660
my complexion answer Corporate America.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:58.070
It entered through the halls of
janitors and cooks and secretaries.

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:02.100
That was the first introduction
to our brilliance.

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:06.300
And so when I, I would say to everyone
when I would look on the 50th floor and

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:10.970
I see Rockefeller I would say, he couldn't
imagine me being the face to his bank.

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:14.757
Having the privilege to
lead the consumer bank, but

00:07:14.757 --> 00:07:20.353
you have to give homage to those who did
not have these amazing rented titles,

00:07:20.353 --> 00:07:23.740
but they showed up with strong character.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:25.950
They showed up with a level of excellence.

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:29.130
They showed up when they
weren't really being seen.

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:33.083
And so when I think about where I am
today, I think about those cooks and

00:07:33.083 --> 00:07:36.299
those janitors I was just
putting cracks in the ceiling,

00:07:36.299 --> 00:07:40.440
that over time I could exist and so
many people like me could exist.

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:45.231
And so I have a lot of respect to those
who've come before me that were enrolls,

00:07:45.231 --> 00:07:46.840
that truly paved the way.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:51.155
&gt;&gt; And I'm sure your leadership
is unnoticed by them, right?

00:07:51.155 --> 00:07:54.480
You literally pulled stories where
you go down in the mailroom and says,

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:56.388
it all starts here, right?

00:07:56.388 --> 00:08:00.682
Absolutely, I mean, look,
when I think about Otis Brown, or

00:08:00.682 --> 00:08:05.381
when I think about so many people,
specifically, people of color,

00:08:05.381 --> 00:08:09.919
many times we are disproportionately
in those lower wage jobs, or

00:08:09.919 --> 00:08:12.374
we are in those entry-level jobs.

00:08:12.374 --> 00:08:17.211
And so, it does not escape me that,
when I have an opportunity to be a CEO or

00:08:17.211 --> 00:08:22.220
to be in a leadership position, to make
sure I go and see those individuals.

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:27.629
To make sure that I can talk about
the strategy and the role that they play.

00:08:27.629 --> 00:08:32.117
And so, when I would go to the mailroom, I
would tell the young individuals that when

00:08:32.117 --> 00:08:36.165
you see us talking about earnings or
talking about that client experience,

00:08:36.165 --> 00:08:39.011
I would say,
I want you to brush your shoulders off and

00:08:39.011 --> 00:08:42.578
know that you had a role to play,
because you started the process.

00:08:42.578 --> 00:08:46.483
You had to do your job with excellence,
when people made their payment in auto,

00:08:46.483 --> 00:08:50.290
to make sure it went to the proper shoot,
so, it would post on time.

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:55.068
And so by connecting them and
understanding that they matter, and

00:08:55.068 --> 00:09:00.523
that they have a voice and that they have
a role to play, what I get in return is

00:09:00.523 --> 00:09:07.090
that the people furthest removed from our
results, see their role in our results.

00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:11.568
And what was so great is, after I was
telling them how awesome they were, I

00:09:11.568 --> 00:09:16.567
remember one of the gentlemen looked at me
and he was like, you welcome we got you.

00:09:16.567 --> 00:09:20.621
[LAUGH] And I just think that's
important because that's Otis Brown.

00:09:20.621 --> 00:09:24.815
My dad worked in a warehouse and he's
brilliant, he didn't go to college, but

00:09:24.815 --> 00:09:27.620
he's brilliant, and he taught me so much.

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:32.502
And I just think it's important to make
sure that we model what it means to see

00:09:32.502 --> 00:09:33.274
everyone.

00:09:33.274 --> 00:09:38.698
Not just in the big town halls, not
just in the employee appreciation notes,

00:09:38.698 --> 00:09:40.956
but in what we do and how we do it.

00:09:40.956 --> 00:09:45.136
And taking the time to engage
with those furthest removed,

00:09:45.136 --> 00:09:49.649
is what really embodies culture,
and what I try to do every day,

00:09:49.649 --> 00:09:54.929
see the unseen, and see the people
that really help, build your company.

00:09:54.929 --> 00:09:58.136
&gt;&gt; Your leadership is jumping
from the screen right now.

00:09:58.136 --> 00:10:00.486
I don't want to cry ten
minutes into this interview.

00:10:00.486 --> 00:10:05.385
So, I think we have a quick video to show
how much folks appreciate your leadership,

00:10:05.385 --> 00:10:06.973
so, we can see [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:08.477 --> 00:10:10.360
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:13.680
&gt;&gt; So inspiring,
I can't even express it to you,

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:18.836
I think it's one thing to be able to
come in this building every Saturday.

00:10:18.836 --> 00:10:21.717
And we're so grateful to be here, but
to be able to see all these black

00:10:21.717 --> 00:10:24.515
entrepreneurs doing so amazing,
but you're the CEO of.

00:10:24.515 --> 00:10:27.428
That's [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK]

00:10:27.428 --> 00:10:30.146
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My god, it is just amazing to see that

00:10:30.146 --> 00:10:33.591
you look like me, and you're able
to do that, and have that impact.

00:10:33.591 --> 00:10:36.171
So, thank you.

00:10:37.766 --> 00:10:42.236
&gt;&gt; I know this was viral a couple
years ago, 2019, but Chloe, I mean,

00:10:42.236 --> 00:10:47.166
I can't Chloe, kills there [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, she's now like my niece.

00:10:47.166 --> 00:10:50.230
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I keep in touch with her,

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:52.389
she's amazing.

00:10:52.389 --> 00:10:57.840
And you're right it went viral,
because it was a real human bowman.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:01.795
And I think it touched a cord
in the art of possibility.

00:11:01.795 --> 00:11:05.745
When people can see themselves in
what it means to be excellent,

00:11:05.745 --> 00:11:08.745
they can then be reminded
that they are worthy and

00:11:08.745 --> 00:11:11.540
deserving of that same
level of excellence.

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:16.751
And I think that's what Chloe and I
represented with the beautiful words that

00:11:16.751 --> 00:11:21.800
she expressed in such a real and human
moment and it was really heartfelt and

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:25.986
I think people could relate to it,
people can understand it.

00:11:25.986 --> 00:11:30.359
And I think, hopefully,
it's a nod in the right direction of

00:11:30.359 --> 00:11:33.902
the work we still need to
do in corporate America.

00:11:33.902 --> 00:11:36.896
And, I think, it's important because
when you look at the stats, right?

00:11:36.896 --> 00:11:41.155
And I know this is a business class,
so I have to try a few stats here-

00:11:41.155 --> 00:11:41.731
&gt;&gt; Let's do it.

00:11:41.731 --> 00:11:46.857
&gt;&gt; It is Stanford, after all, but
I was looking at the Fortune 500.

00:11:46.857 --> 00:11:49.844
And when we talk about all the work,
that we do,

00:11:49.844 --> 00:11:54.276
all the initiatives that are out there,
we have to be outcome driven.

00:11:54.276 --> 00:12:01.503
And the reality is there's only, I think,
41 women, that are leading a fortune 500,

00:12:01.503 --> 00:12:06.335
there are 16 Latinx, and
there were 3 African Americans.

00:12:06.335 --> 00:12:06.858
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:12:06.858 --> 00:12:12.165
&gt;&gt; And so, we can talk about all the
things that we're doing, but we have to

00:12:12.165 --> 00:12:18.442
ask ourselves if we believe that talent is
created equally, and opportunity is not.

00:12:18.442 --> 00:12:21.098
It bears out when you
look at the shape and

00:12:21.098 --> 00:12:25.330
the profile of those who
are leading Fortune 500 companies.

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:28.478
Which says to me,
we still have a lot of work to do.

00:12:28.478 --> 00:12:33.316
And it really, I think, hits a nerve
when you see the video, Chloe and

00:12:33.316 --> 00:12:36.107
I having a moment, because it matters.

00:12:36.107 --> 00:12:39.424
And, clearly, the outcomes are not
bearing what we believe and

00:12:39.424 --> 00:12:41.399
what we aspire to be true as a country.

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:46.361
&gt;&gt; For sure, I love the stats and
the backs, I think they ground us.

00:12:46.361 --> 00:12:50.255
The one that I wanted to mention since you
are very vocal on financial equity and

00:12:50.255 --> 00:12:53.690
health for everyone, not just leaders-
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:57.560
&gt;&gt; Is the median net worth of
black single men and women,

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:00.140
versus single white men and women.

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:04.696
I'll let you share the information but,
I think that's important to return to

00:13:04.696 --> 00:13:08.844
the numbers, and remember we fight for
progress and want progress, but

00:13:08.844 --> 00:13:12.255
there's still some very
harrowing statistics out there.

00:13:12.255 --> 00:13:16.157
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and
I think when you look at the numbers,

00:13:16.157 --> 00:13:20.546
it tells us what all we still
have to do to make sure that

00:13:20.546 --> 00:13:25.149
there's full inclusion and
equity for all Americans.

00:13:25.149 --> 00:13:30.779
And so what you're referencing is the
median net worth of single black men and

00:13:30.779 --> 00:13:35.283
women are like 200 to $300
versus white men and women,

00:13:35.283 --> 00:13:38.329
which is around 15 to $28,000.

00:13:38.329 --> 00:13:42.780
And so when you think about that,
we have to say, what is going on?

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:46.768
And we have to take a moment and
say, what has to change?

00:13:46.768 --> 00:13:48.050
And what do we do?

00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:51.150
What are those choke points
that we have to unlock?

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:55.133
Because, clearly,
as a society, as a human race,

00:13:55.133 --> 00:13:58.309
we cannot be okay with those statistics.

00:13:58.309 --> 00:14:01.837
And we have to know that we can
do better and that, surely,

00:14:01.837 --> 00:14:05.710
it cannot be all of the melanin
that's causing these results.

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:09.674
It's not the melanin,
there's something else that's going on,

00:14:09.674 --> 00:14:13.846
that we have to lead boldly and
be provocative about these stats, but,

00:14:13.846 --> 00:14:18.040
more importantly, be bold about what
we can do better to move forward.

00:14:19.300 --> 00:14:22.520
&gt;&gt; As a starting point,
maybe you mentioned in a TED talk, right?

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:24.693
We just need to talk about money, period.

00:14:24.693 --> 00:14:27.213
And you told the story money's personal,

00:14:27.213 --> 00:14:31.915
there's a lot of stuff that comes with it
when you bring it up in a conversation.

00:14:31.915 --> 00:14:32.796
But you told the story, right?

00:14:32.796 --> 00:14:37.483
Of your dad and the 401k, and
just the knowledge of knowing that

00:14:37.483 --> 00:14:42.518
existed perhaps could have changed
the financial situation, right?

00:14:42.518 --> 00:14:46.809
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean, when I think about my
father, and I think about my parents, and

00:14:46.809 --> 00:14:49.814
I think about all the sacrifices
that they made for us.

00:14:49.814 --> 00:14:53.085
And I remember after I
graduated from college and

00:14:53.085 --> 00:14:57.630
I was looking at my dad's statement,
and to have to tell your father

00:14:57.630 --> 00:15:02.202
that you don't have enough,
not even close, to be able to retire.

00:15:02.202 --> 00:15:06.586
And what angered me is that
the 401k was for him, but

00:15:06.586 --> 00:15:09.610
he didn't see it within himself.

00:15:09.610 --> 00:15:14.791
So, the information or the way in
which the information was shared,

00:15:14.791 --> 00:15:17.217
he didn't think it was for him.

00:15:17.217 --> 00:15:19.885
And so, when you think about
these opportunities, and

00:15:19.885 --> 00:15:22.953
you think about minorities
participating in the stock market or

00:15:22.953 --> 00:15:25.018
understanding the power of compounding.

00:15:25.018 --> 00:15:27.687
Or you think about
the homeownership rates, or

00:15:27.687 --> 00:15:30.955
you think about small businesses,
you can go on, and on,

00:15:30.955 --> 00:15:36.390
all of the things that can unlock wealth,
generational wealth, there's more to it.

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:41.912
The information may be out there, but can
people see themselves, are we making it

00:15:41.912 --> 00:15:47.448
where folks do not have to feel as if, I
didn't go to college, so it's not for me.

00:15:47.448 --> 00:15:50.091
Or this was something
that wasn't discussed,

00:15:50.091 --> 00:15:52.145
and I don't want to seem less smart.

00:15:52.145 --> 00:15:57.868
And so, I think the opportunity for us,
is to really make sure That we tap into,

00:15:57.868 --> 00:16:00.784
not just the information,
but how it's presented.

00:16:00.784 --> 00:16:04.070
And you're right, I talk about
it in a TED talk because I said,

00:16:04.070 --> 00:16:06.930
we have to talk about this
without shame or judgement.

00:16:06.930 --> 00:16:09.660
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; No shame, no judgement.

00:16:09.660 --> 00:16:12.261
It's not where you start,
it's where you end up.

00:16:12.261 --> 00:16:18.093
And whether it's $1 a day or a quarter,
just get going and get started.

00:16:18.093 --> 00:16:21.845
Because that power of compounding and
that behavior, over time,

00:16:21.845 --> 00:16:25.537
can really mean the difference
when you think about your wealth.

00:16:25.537 --> 00:16:29.471
But, clearly, a lot of times
when people see their finances,

00:16:29.471 --> 00:16:34.070
they associate their financial
situation to their self-worth.

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:39.310
And I like to say, your net worth
does not define your self-worth.

00:16:39.310 --> 00:16:41.872
And I just think that's so
important and we have to be okay.

00:16:41.872 --> 00:16:45.890
I like to say, we know how to keep it 100,
especially, young people.

00:16:45.890 --> 00:16:48.260
But we have to know how to keep it 700+.

00:16:48.260 --> 00:16:52.182
We have to know how to get started and
be okay with where we are, but saying,

00:16:52.182 --> 00:16:56.190
hey, we can make these steps to have
a better outcome and a better tomorrow.

00:16:57.810 --> 00:17:00.620
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, it's a great message.

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:04.645
It's a scary, I think, thought for
folks to make that initial plunge.

00:17:04.645 --> 00:17:06.413
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; Hopefully we can get more,

00:17:06.413 --> 00:17:10.524
us minorities taking the plunge,
understanding, as you said,

00:17:10.524 --> 00:17:15.851
the power of compounding, and hopefully
a more financially sound future for them.

00:17:15.851 --> 00:17:16.970
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:17:18.713 --> 00:17:22.689
&gt;&gt; And beyond financial equity,
it's obviously been a tough year for

00:17:22.689 --> 00:17:24.370
a lot of different reasons.

00:17:25.590 --> 00:17:27.638
I don't want to just ask about race,
outright.

00:17:27.638 --> 00:17:30.700
I think when you were talking
to the mayor of Arlington, and

00:17:30.700 --> 00:17:34.621
I know Arlington is a super important
place for you, you said something that

00:17:34.621 --> 00:17:38.960
actually it hit me pretty deep,
which is extraordinary is kindness.

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:40.250
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:44.207
&gt;&gt; So, if we're just kinder with
each other, and we're more patient,

00:17:44.207 --> 00:17:48.165
perhaps that's the starting point
to ending some of these tensions,

00:17:48.165 --> 00:17:53.110
which seem to be wrapped up in other
things the news likes to highlight.

00:17:53.110 --> 00:17:54.380
&gt;&gt; No, I think you're right.

00:17:54.380 --> 00:17:59.640
And I like to say, what it means to be
extraordinary, are your ownable assets.

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:02.820
Extraordinary is kindness,
extraordinary is laughter.

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:06.979
I also think extraordinary
is doing the work.

00:18:06.979 --> 00:18:11.840
And so many times when we have these
conversations, particularly around race or

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:15.690
inequality, we want to start
in the middle of a chapter.

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:20.160
But I think the way in which we can
unlock and make real progress, is to get

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:26.080
proximate with the history, and to really
understand the structural inequities.

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:30.271
To really understand what it means when
you grew up in a segregated south.

00:18:30.271 --> 00:18:34.089
To really understand books
like first school, and

00:18:34.089 --> 00:18:39.874
the history that we have in our society
that has caused so many inequalities.

00:18:39.874 --> 00:18:44.124
And it's not, again,
to make people feel bad or ashamed,

00:18:44.124 --> 00:18:46.400
but it is to get to the root.

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:51.023
In business, as you know, in business
school, you have to get to the root cause,

00:18:51.023 --> 00:18:52.440
the root cause.

00:18:52.440 --> 00:18:53.700
And that's what you do in business.

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:56.486
When you see something you say,
what is the root cause?

00:18:56.486 --> 00:18:58.080
The root cause analysis.

00:18:58.080 --> 00:19:03.021
And so I think that if we can get to
the root of the matter, and get educated,

00:19:03.021 --> 00:19:06.786
and get proximate, and
do it with a headset of kindness,

00:19:06.786 --> 00:19:10.250
a headset of saying, I want what you want.

00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:15.780
I want my children to breathe just like
you want your children to breathe.

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:20.287
I want a full opportunity just like
you want a full opportunity, and

00:19:20.287 --> 00:19:22.120
then we do the work.

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:24.876
And I do think that if we
work on ourselves, and

00:19:24.876 --> 00:19:28.268
do it with kindness, and
do it with a level of curiosity,

00:19:28.268 --> 00:19:32.040
then it's us that become
the people in positions of power.

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:35.288
It is us that can then be that mom,
at a school and

00:19:35.288 --> 00:19:37.660
volunteering and doing the work.

00:19:37.660 --> 00:19:41.926
It is us, it is humanity that
will unlock the progress.

00:19:41.926 --> 00:19:42.819
Policies and

00:19:42.819 --> 00:19:48.870
everything else will change when it's the
people that decide that we can do better.

00:19:48.870 --> 00:19:52.878
And I think kindness is at
the heart of everything that we do,

00:19:52.878 --> 00:19:55.010
when you think about progress.

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:58.375
&gt;&gt; That's incredible, if the people
decide, and kindness will be unlocked,

00:19:58.375 --> 00:19:59.524
everything will follow.

00:19:59.524 --> 00:20:01.724
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:20:01.724 --> 00:20:02.550
&gt;&gt; That would be ideal.

00:20:02.550 --> 00:20:04.190
&gt;&gt; That's the work, right?

00:20:04.190 --> 00:20:07.610
That's the good trouble we
have to get into, and stay in.

00:20:07.610 --> 00:20:08.491
&gt;&gt; It's a fight worth fighting.

00:20:08.491 --> 00:20:10.164
But as you said, it's not new.

00:20:10.164 --> 00:20:13.850
There have been many people before
us that have engaged in this.

00:20:13.850 --> 00:20:17.190
We need to learn from those lessons and
continue to work forward.

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:20.245
&gt;&gt; The thing that I think is so
powerful about that is that,

00:20:20.245 --> 00:20:23.440
if we're honest with ourselves,
we have made progress.

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:30.190
And it is easier than it was for
my grandparents.

00:20:30.190 --> 00:20:34.930
It is easier than it was from those
who enslaved into the country.

00:20:34.930 --> 00:20:38.820
So, progress is slow,
progress does not go in a straight line.

00:20:38.820 --> 00:20:42.680
It goes up and down, but
it's progress, all the same.

00:20:42.680 --> 00:20:46.975
And so, I think that it's important
that when we are in positions of power,

00:20:46.975 --> 00:20:48.934
that we understand the platform.

00:20:48.934 --> 00:20:54.430
And that we understand that it's more than
just delivering on the financial results.

00:20:54.430 --> 00:20:56.085
It is about shaping a culture.

00:20:56.085 --> 00:20:59.570
It is about using our voice to
make real progress in our country,

00:20:59.570 --> 00:21:01.710
and I think that's good for business.

00:21:01.710 --> 00:21:03.109
It's good for community.

00:21:03.109 --> 00:21:05.732
But is also, for me,
why I believe I'm in this [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:05.732 --> 00:21:09.852
It is to stay in that good trouble and
to create that art of possibility,

00:21:09.852 --> 00:21:14.247
so that more people can ascend, and
really see the power that they have, but

00:21:14.247 --> 00:21:17.151
also be rewarded with their hard work and
effort.

00:21:17.151 --> 00:21:20.697
And so progress is slow,
but we are making progress.

00:21:20.697 --> 00:21:22.234
And I think we have to stay hopeful.

00:21:22.234 --> 00:21:23.970
At least, I'm hopeful, I have no choice.

00:21:23.970 --> 00:21:25.277
I have a lot of kids.

00:21:25.277 --> 00:21:30.135
And I have to stay hopeful and believe
that they will have better days than I and

00:21:30.135 --> 00:21:31.268
those before me.

00:21:31.268 --> 00:21:33.182
&gt;&gt; Under your leadership,
I'm sure they will, and

00:21:33.182 --> 00:21:35.130
they'll be leaders themselves, very soon.

00:21:35.130 --> 00:21:35.926
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:21:35.926 --> 00:21:41.098
&gt;&gt; So, Shonda, I want to end with
a couple questions that are directly for

00:21:41.098 --> 00:21:42.861
the Class of 2021.

00:21:42.861 --> 00:21:45.304
They have 20 days till they graduate.

00:21:45.304 --> 00:21:48.461
So we'll look for some advice from
you on a couple different topics.

00:21:48.461 --> 00:21:49.438
&gt;&gt; Okay, great.

00:21:49.438 --> 00:21:56.940
&gt;&gt; Amazing, so, firstly, folks they love
the titles, the gold-plated careers.

00:21:56.940 --> 00:22:01.307
But as you said earlier, you rent your
title and you own your character, right?

00:22:01.307 --> 00:22:02.419
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:22:02.419 --> 00:22:05.668
&gt;&gt; So, what advice, and
you made this decision for yourself,

00:22:05.668 --> 00:22:07.911
choosing the right position over money.

00:22:07.911 --> 00:22:10.553
What advice would you give folks that are,
perhaps,

00:22:10.553 --> 00:22:12.617
grappling with that decision right now?

00:22:12.617 --> 00:22:15.951
&gt;&gt; Well I'm glad you brought that
point because I do think I want you

00:22:15.951 --> 00:22:20.510
all to write down and to know that you
rent your title, you own your character.

00:22:20.510 --> 00:22:23.170
So you should show what
was your ownable assets.

00:22:23.170 --> 00:22:27.888
And so when you're thinking about the job
and you're thinking about the opportunity,

00:22:27.888 --> 00:22:31.073
what I would say is,
make sure that whatever role you take or

00:22:31.073 --> 00:22:33.644
whatever position, understand the culture.

00:22:33.644 --> 00:22:36.191
Understand who you are.

00:22:36.191 --> 00:22:40.340
Understand and know that you
have the power to make progress.

00:22:40.340 --> 00:22:45.148
And I also think that it's important to
make sure that, whatever road you travel,

00:22:45.148 --> 00:22:49.887
to stay intellectually curious, and even
if you take a role that may not be ideal,

00:22:49.887 --> 00:22:53.260
understand that there's
a lesson in that moment.

00:22:53.260 --> 00:22:55.250
There's a lesson in that journey.

00:22:55.250 --> 00:22:59.170
And so, it's not so
much about which job you take or what,

00:22:59.170 --> 00:23:04.213
just make sure that whatever you decide,
stick with it, take the lesson,

00:23:04.213 --> 00:23:08.777
and know that I would rather take
those lessons getting started, so

00:23:08.777 --> 00:23:13.852
that when you level up, you're prepared,
because you've been tested.

00:23:13.852 --> 00:23:17.390
Versus running from job to job,
the minute it gets a little tough, or

00:23:17.390 --> 00:23:20.440
you don't like something,
then how are you going to grow?

00:23:20.440 --> 00:23:24.562
And then how are you going to have
a perspective to be able to tell someone

00:23:24.562 --> 00:23:26.690
else something along the way?

00:23:26.690 --> 00:23:29.030
So, rent your title, own your character.

00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:32.483
When you think about your role,
stay curious, stick with it, and

00:23:32.483 --> 00:23:35.998
think about what's the lesson that
you're supposed to learn, and

00:23:35.998 --> 00:23:39.530
what is the gift that you're supposed
to give to that environment?

00:23:39.530 --> 00:23:42.421
&gt;&gt; Strong start, I like all that advice,
I'm going to follow it myself.

00:23:42.421 --> 00:23:44.630
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:23:44.630 --> 00:23:45.521
&gt;&gt; How about a mentorship?

00:23:45.521 --> 00:23:49.978
So you've mentioned before, perhaps you
don't learn as much as you think from

00:23:49.978 --> 00:23:52.715
the person seven levels
above you in the C suite.

00:23:52.715 --> 00:23:55.955
Perhaps it's the people around you
that you should be looking for, for

00:23:55.955 --> 00:23:56.713
the learnings.

00:23:56.713 --> 00:23:57.866
What did you mean by that?

00:23:57.866 --> 00:23:58.580
&gt;&gt; My gosh.

00:23:58.580 --> 00:24:02.260
So, the minute we get into corporate
America, all of a sudden we're like,

00:24:02.260 --> 00:24:05.684
I need these mentors that
are already in these big rolls,

00:24:05.684 --> 00:24:09.840
but I like to remind people
mentorship is all around you.

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:11.400
It's all around you.

00:24:11.400 --> 00:24:15.240
And what I mean by that, is when you
think about who you are, there's so

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:17.060
many facets of you.

00:24:17.060 --> 00:24:21.470
So how could one person
give you that entire gift?

00:24:21.470 --> 00:24:23.810
So I get mentored by sister.

00:24:23.810 --> 00:24:30.310
She shows me the power of service of how
she shows up on my behalf with excellence.

00:24:30.310 --> 00:24:33.253
I am mentored by having this
opportunity with the youth,

00:24:33.253 --> 00:24:36.480
the people that are going
to do amazing things.

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:39.130
And so
my point is mentorship is all around you.

00:24:39.130 --> 00:24:41.510
It's in the history books that you read.

00:24:41.510 --> 00:24:46.533
It's in the observations of the youth,
it's in senior leaders as well,

00:24:46.533 --> 00:24:51.476
but the most important thing is to
understand that if you are truly trying

00:24:51.476 --> 00:24:56.337
to hone your skills, then you should
hone that skill of not limiting who

00:24:56.337 --> 00:24:59.420
mentors you by their rented title.

00:24:59.420 --> 00:25:03.929
But who is showing up in a way that can
teach you something that can iron your

00:25:03.929 --> 00:25:07.491
hone your skill set in a way that
you can be a better human and

00:25:07.491 --> 00:25:10.070
I just think mentorship is all around us.

00:25:10.070 --> 00:25:11.771
You have to just open up your eyes and

00:25:11.771 --> 00:25:15.020
be willing to receive it in whichever
form that it presents itself.

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:17.560
&gt;&gt; Absolutely and

00:25:17.560 --> 00:25:22.457
I think closely on that topic around
looking to the folks around you for

00:25:22.457 --> 00:25:28.400
leadership and for inspiration, your
friends and perhaps your partner as well.

00:25:28.400 --> 00:25:33.060
I think we have a couple
of pictures to show

00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:34.955
&gt;&gt; You guys did your work.

00:25:34.955 --> 00:25:36.872
[LAUGH].

00:25:36.872 --> 00:25:38.620
&gt;&gt; I love the fact check those comments.

00:25:38.620 --> 00:25:41.800
There, key to a long relationship
flirt with your husband.

00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:44.330
&gt;&gt; I love that.

00:25:45.620 --> 00:25:49.048
&gt;&gt; Best board CEO way to lay except that
the most important business decision you

00:25:49.048 --> 00:25:50.890
make is your partner,
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:52.980
&gt;&gt; I understand you guys have
a wonderful relationship

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:54.180
&gt;&gt; Me too.

00:25:54.180 --> 00:25:57.198
&gt;&gt; What advice would you give
the MBAs on their friends and

00:25:57.198 --> 00:25:59.307
if you want their partner selection?

00:25:59.307 --> 00:26:02.577
&gt;&gt; Well, I love this question, because so

00:26:02.577 --> 00:26:07.481
many times when we think about
a relationship, you may say,

00:26:07.481 --> 00:26:13.510
I want him to do this, and I want him
to do that or this or that for me.

00:26:13.510 --> 00:26:15.460
But it's the matters of the heart.

00:26:15.460 --> 00:26:19.140
And the reality is I have a man
who is anchored in character.

00:26:19.140 --> 00:26:23.275
I have a man who believes that
his role is to lighten my load,

00:26:23.275 --> 00:26:27.520
who understands that I
want to impact the world.

00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:31.120
And so as a result,
he's an engineer and a marine.

00:26:31.120 --> 00:26:33.350
But he's a stay at home, dad.

00:26:33.350 --> 00:26:36.165
He runs our family office I like to say.

00:26:36.165 --> 00:26:40.339
[LAUGH] And Buddy, but what I just
think it's about a partnership and

00:26:40.339 --> 00:26:45.100
it's about those ownable assets and so
whomever you choose to spend your time

00:26:45.100 --> 00:26:48.700
with, whether it's in life or
in friendships.

00:26:48.700 --> 00:26:53.250
Think about the character, think about
the ownable assets because that's really

00:26:53.250 --> 00:26:57.610
what's going to help you navigate
your storms, your ups and your downs.

00:26:57.610 --> 00:27:02.410
And I'm just incredibly blessed that I
have a partner who truly is my partner

00:27:02.410 --> 00:27:03.070
in life.

00:27:03.070 --> 00:27:07.800
And most importantly, he's on this journey
to help me be the best version of myself

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:09.530
that I can possibly be.

00:27:09.530 --> 00:27:14.490
And he's additive to who I am and
being able to unlock all of who I am when

00:27:14.490 --> 00:27:19.034
it comes to being a mother,
a philanthropist, an executive and

00:27:19.034 --> 00:27:22.670
all the other titles that
are part of my portfolio.

00:27:23.980 --> 00:27:24.990
&gt;&gt; That's incredible.

00:27:24.990 --> 00:27:25.880
I love to hear that.

00:27:25.880 --> 00:27:28.070
I think it's wonderful advice.

00:27:28.070 --> 00:27:32.360
Find someone additive, who supports you,
who makes a better person, right?

00:27:32.360 --> 00:27:34.190
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, absolutely.

00:27:35.370 --> 00:27:36.170
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:27:36.170 --> 00:27:39.420
One more on the career side and
then we'll give you our final question.

00:27:39.420 --> 00:27:42.950
So, obviously you took the new role at TI.

00:27:42.950 --> 00:27:45.204
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; What advice would you give to folks

00:27:45.204 --> 00:27:49.362
as they make these decisions craps,
they love the rule they read before but

00:27:49.362 --> 00:27:50.814
now opportunity arises,

00:27:50.814 --> 00:27:54.843
what is kind of your thought process and
how do you make those decisions?

00:27:54.843 --> 00:28:00.150
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's such a great question
because I absolutely loved my prior role.

00:28:00.150 --> 00:28:03.430
I loved the company, the people.

00:28:03.430 --> 00:28:06.080
I felt like I still had a lot of runway.

00:28:07.400 --> 00:28:10.300
But I also believe in
the art of possibility, and

00:28:10.300 --> 00:28:13.030
I believe in getting uncomfortable.

00:28:13.030 --> 00:28:19.380
And clearly, if there was a word to
describe 2020, it was uncomfortability.

00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:24.061
But what was beautiful is when you
sit down in your quiet moments and

00:28:24.061 --> 00:28:28.409
you ask yourself,
are you reaching your highest potential,

00:28:28.409 --> 00:28:32.686
not from a title perspective,
from an impact perspective.

00:28:32.686 --> 00:28:37.670
And when I realised that I wanted to
be open to the art of possibility

00:28:37.670 --> 00:28:42.565
that maybe I can show up in a way
that could be even more impactful,

00:28:42.565 --> 00:28:45.320
TIAA came knocking at the door.

00:28:45.320 --> 00:28:51.736
And now what I can say in hindsight, of
course, is that to be able to be the CEO,

00:28:51.736 --> 00:28:58.345
a TIAA full circle when I think about my
parents, to be able to be the first woman,

00:28:58.345 --> 00:29:04.410
third African American to make history
in so many ways, that's impact.

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:08.980
I like to say, sometimes we say,
we're trying to crack the ceiling.

00:29:08.980 --> 00:29:10.598
Forget the ceiling, just step outside.

00:29:10.598 --> 00:29:14.168
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] Sometimes we just have to

00:29:14.168 --> 00:29:20.570
get out of our box of comfortability and
know that we are worthy and deserving.

00:29:21.950 --> 00:29:27.330
And taking a risk is not a risk
if your objective is impact.

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:30.188
If your objective is something
higher than yourself,

00:29:30.188 --> 00:29:34.500
then you have to be okay with getting
uncomfortable and then going for it.

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:35.310
Going for it.

00:29:35.310 --> 00:29:37.190
Listen to that quiet voice.

00:29:37.190 --> 00:29:39.050
Do the work, be prepared.

00:29:39.050 --> 00:29:41.480
Don't leave when you're frustrated or mad.

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:43.700
Make sure you have a clear mind and
a clear heart.

00:29:44.790 --> 00:29:48.310
And listen to what could be there for you.

00:29:48.310 --> 00:29:49.150
And be okay with it.

00:29:49.150 --> 00:29:50.260
What's the worst that could happen?

00:29:52.500 --> 00:29:55.376
&gt;&gt; Pretty good advice,
another set of incredible lines,

00:29:55.376 --> 00:29:57.160
forget the ceiling step outside.

00:29:57.160 --> 00:29:59.190
It seems like you've
practiced this before.

00:29:59.190 --> 00:30:00.940
&gt;&gt; No hey, it's just real.

00:30:00.940 --> 00:30:01.940
It's so real.

00:30:01.940 --> 00:30:05.173
And I try to I always say
model the behavior you aspire,

00:30:05.173 --> 00:30:07.570
just don't talk about it, be about it.

00:30:07.570 --> 00:30:13.330
And so for me, when I'm sharing with
all of you is what I've experienced.

00:30:13.330 --> 00:30:18.118
It's how I make sure that I'm
always stepping into larger and

00:30:18.118 --> 00:30:22.710
larger containers and
not feeling that limited.

00:30:22.710 --> 00:30:26.098
And sometimes, especially as a woman,
as a black woman,

00:30:26.098 --> 00:30:28.830
those limitations can feel heavy.

00:30:28.830 --> 00:30:32.617
And that's when you have to say when
people are are saying things or

00:30:32.617 --> 00:30:36.742
maybe marginalizing you, you have to
do like when you receive something

00:30:36.742 --> 00:30:41.110
in the mail and it's not addressed to you,
you return that to sender.

00:30:41.110 --> 00:30:44.939
And so, if people are coming for
me on things that are not me or

00:30:44.939 --> 00:30:47.810
do not define me,
I'm not going to own that.

00:30:47.810 --> 00:30:51.317
I'm just going to politely just
return that back to sender,

00:30:51.317 --> 00:30:52.980
because that is not who I am.

00:30:52.980 --> 00:30:55.190
And that means I'm limitless.

00:30:55.190 --> 00:30:57.430
And that means if I have to step outside,
that's what I will do.

00:30:58.760 --> 00:30:59.980
&gt;&gt; Incredible.

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:01.107
One last question for

00:31:01.107 --> 00:31:04.242
you to Shawn that would ask us
to all the speakers this year.

00:31:04.242 --> 00:31:06.450
In this challenging moment.

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:11.265
In these tough times, what principles
do you rely on as a leader?

00:31:11.265 --> 00:31:16.760
&gt;&gt; Wow, I mean first my faith
is really important to me.

00:31:16.760 --> 00:31:23.995
My character,
knowing that it's okay to not be okay,

00:31:23.995 --> 00:31:28.570
knowing that this too shall pass.

00:31:30.810 --> 00:31:35.200
Knowing that in our vulnerability
can be the unlocks of possibility.

00:31:36.920 --> 00:31:42.768
And I think as a leader,
this notion of being curious,

00:31:42.768 --> 00:31:47.000
not just from a business sense.

00:31:47.000 --> 00:31:49.244
But I want to know you.

00:31:49.244 --> 00:31:51.641
I want to know what motivates you.

00:31:51.641 --> 00:31:56.078
I want to know what makes your
heart heavy, because as a leader,

00:31:56.078 --> 00:32:01.267
I think if I can show my vulnerability,
then I create permission for you to

00:32:01.267 --> 00:32:06.350
show yours and it's in that vulnerability
That allows us to take a breath.

00:32:07.580 --> 00:32:10.720
And that allows us to no
longer wear the mask.

00:32:10.720 --> 00:32:16.180
Paul Laurence Dunbar wrote a poem in 1913
Wear the Mask, we can take the mask off.

00:32:17.280 --> 00:32:20.010
And then we can really focus on the work.

00:32:20.010 --> 00:32:24.717
And I think that's so important when
I think about leadership principles,

00:32:24.717 --> 00:32:28.615
character, curiosity,
being vulnerable, authentic, and

00:32:28.615 --> 00:32:33.024
just knowing that the goal for me is
to be the best version of myself, and

00:32:33.024 --> 00:32:35.622
that's what I expect for everyone else.

00:32:35.622 --> 00:32:39.201
And in order for you to be the best
version of you, have to create space for

00:32:39.201 --> 00:32:41.032
you to do that, unapologetically.

00:32:42.566 --> 00:32:45.630
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much Shonda for
chatting right now.

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:47.460
It's been a complete pleasure.

00:32:47.460 --> 00:32:49.630
What a wonderful message to leave with.

00:32:49.630 --> 00:32:53.209
We have 2 student questions, and
I know my classmates are incredible,

00:32:53.209 --> 00:32:56.020
so they're going to raise
the bar even higher.

00:32:56.020 --> 00:32:59.460
I'm excited to invite
them to join [CROSSTALK]

00:32:59.460 --> 00:33:00.067
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:33:00.067 --> 00:33:02.706
&gt;&gt; Hi.
I can't believe I'm talking

00:33:02.706 --> 00:33:04.025
to you right now.

00:33:04.025 --> 00:33:05.968
This is amazing.

00:33:05.968 --> 00:33:10.253
I worked at JPMorgan Chase in
operations for the last 3 and

00:33:10.253 --> 00:33:12.620
a half years, so I know you as T.

00:33:12.620 --> 00:33:15.093
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Thank you T for joining, I'm so

00:33:15.093 --> 00:33:15.760
excited.

00:33:17.020 --> 00:33:19.490
Okay, so
we talked about this already a little bit.

00:33:19.490 --> 00:33:22.970
And so maybe I'll position this
question a little bit more tactically.

00:33:22.970 --> 00:33:28.461
But, one of the things that has always
struck me have been your anecdotes about,

00:33:28.461 --> 00:33:32.440
leading people of all levels
in an enormous institution.

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:37.260
And the way you've done that is to build
those relationships in the mailroom etc.

00:33:38.280 --> 00:33:41.098
So I am pivoting to
a career in hospitality,

00:33:41.098 --> 00:33:44.318
which is a big shift from
operations at JPMorgan.

00:33:44.318 --> 00:33:48.988
[LAUGH] And as someone who's seen you
model this behavior so well, I'm wondering

00:33:48.988 --> 00:33:53.794
what tactics you could share about how to
build that credibility with those folks,

00:33:53.794 --> 00:33:56.747
when you're education
levels are different, and

00:33:56.747 --> 00:34:01.030
your perspectives are different, but
you're all critical to the work.

00:34:02.170 --> 00:34:05.320
So yeah, I just love your thoughts and
thanks again.

00:34:05.320 --> 00:34:07.920
&gt;&gt; First of all, thank you so
much Lauren, right.

00:34:07.920 --> 00:34:09.050
Is it Lauren?

00:34:09.050 --> 00:34:11.810
Okay, great.
Well, it's so great to meet you Lauren.

00:34:11.810 --> 00:34:16.110
First I would say congratulations
on deciding that you want to pivot.

00:34:16.110 --> 00:34:17.773
That's not easy.

00:34:17.773 --> 00:34:19.706
I can feel it.
Your like, I don't know, but

00:34:19.706 --> 00:34:21.430
you really want to do it.

00:34:21.430 --> 00:34:26.670
Here's what you need to remember Lauren,
show with your ownable assets.

00:34:26.670 --> 00:34:32.060
What makes you special, is that you have
your own story and your own narrative.

00:34:32.060 --> 00:34:35.200
You worked in operations,
so you understand process.

00:34:35.200 --> 00:34:39.470
You transfer that into hospitality,
that is really important.

00:34:39.470 --> 00:34:44.338
The process, the change management,
the different roles, what people do and

00:34:44.338 --> 00:34:48.780
how that impacts the presentment
of ultimately whatever that is.

00:34:48.780 --> 00:34:50.016
You have that muscle.

00:34:50.016 --> 00:34:54.456
And so, you may need to just be reminded
that the work that you put in over

00:34:54.456 --> 00:34:57.835
the years is preparing you for
this moment.

00:34:57.835 --> 00:35:02.315
Understanding the back office, and
understanding how important it is.

00:35:02.315 --> 00:35:07.435
It's so critical in hospitality, because
you'll see the people that are cleaning

00:35:07.435 --> 00:35:12.196
the rooms or preparing the meals or
doing the landscaping, and you understand

00:35:12.196 --> 00:35:17.170
that they are so important, to
the customer facing part of the equation.

00:35:17.170 --> 00:35:21.761
And then lastly, bet on yourself,
and show up with your character,

00:35:21.761 --> 00:35:26.909
show up with your intellectual curiosity,
show up with your humble spirit,

00:35:26.909 --> 00:35:30.393
show up with that smile,
show up with that level of,

00:35:30.393 --> 00:35:35.720
I don't know anything about it, but I'm so
excited to show up with all of that.

00:35:35.720 --> 00:35:39.259
Because that gets that expression,
that look you just gave,

00:35:39.259 --> 00:35:43.483
that's what's warming,
that's what's inviting, and ultimately,

00:35:43.483 --> 00:35:47.520
that's where your magic lies,
that's where your superpower lies.

00:35:47.520 --> 00:35:48.570
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much.

00:35:48.570 --> 00:35:50.595
I feel so known in 2 minutes.

00:35:50.595 --> 00:35:53.410
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] It's real though,
it really is real.

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:54.570
So good luck to you.

00:35:54.570 --> 00:35:55.825
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
You look fantastic

00:35:55.825 --> 00:35:57.993
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:35:57.993 --> 00:35:59.031
&gt;&gt; Hi T.

00:35:59.031 --> 00:35:59.870
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:35:59.870 --> 00:36:01.090
&gt;&gt; It's an honor you're here with us.

00:36:02.170 --> 00:36:06.700
Like Chloe, I am in awe and I'm just so
grateful to see you on the screen.

00:36:06.700 --> 00:36:08.550
So I really appreciate that.

00:36:08.550 --> 00:36:13.135
I actually have to give credit to this
question to one of my classmates who

00:36:13.135 --> 00:36:14.798
inspired it, Danielle.

00:36:14.798 --> 00:36:19.043
And my question is,
sometimes if there is a CEO who is from

00:36:19.043 --> 00:36:23.555
an underrepresented background,
or who is a woman, Q&amp;A,

00:36:23.555 --> 00:36:28.380
his fireside chats,
etc often focus on that identity.

00:36:28.380 --> 00:36:31.457
And I mean,
we've talked about this already today, and

00:36:31.457 --> 00:36:33.260
these questions are important.

00:36:33.260 --> 00:36:38.100
But I also imagine that it can be somewhat
draining to have to answer what it's

00:36:38.100 --> 00:36:41.301
like to be a black woman
in your role sometimes, and

00:36:41.301 --> 00:36:43.474
other CEOs don't have to do that.

00:36:43.474 --> 00:36:48.680
So I'm curious to hear from you,
what question do we not ask you enough?

00:36:48.680 --> 00:36:51.575
And if you're willing,
could you answer that question for us?

00:36:51.575 --> 00:36:55.440
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Wow, what a powerful question.

00:36:55.440 --> 00:36:57.740
You said Danielle, I gave you that.

00:36:57.740 --> 00:37:01.350
Well, shout out to Danielle, and
to you Chelsea for asking it.

00:37:01.350 --> 00:37:05.230
Let me attack it in a couple of ways.

00:37:05.230 --> 00:37:11.038
First and foremost the reality is, being
black has shaped so much of who I am.

00:37:11.038 --> 00:37:13.614
It has.

00:37:13.614 --> 00:37:18.800
And the reality is,
I feel privileged to have the opportunity

00:37:18.800 --> 00:37:23.089
to talk about being black or
being a woman because,

00:37:23.089 --> 00:37:27.974
I'm trying to get the world to
get proximate to who we are,

00:37:27.974 --> 00:37:30.966
so that we can change the outcome and

00:37:30.966 --> 00:37:36.285
the narrative of what is possible,
and to show that we belong.

00:37:36.285 --> 00:37:40.863
And so, I am more than happy to
carry the burden if you will,

00:37:40.863 --> 00:37:44.080
because it's not for them it's for you.

00:37:46.040 --> 00:37:47.860
That's what it's for.

00:37:47.860 --> 00:37:53.569
And I know that so many women, and so
many black women still feel invisible.

00:37:54.750 --> 00:37:58.309
And so,
if I chose to ignore that question,

00:37:58.309 --> 00:38:02.734
or chose to just talk about
return on capital or SBA, or

00:38:02.734 --> 00:38:07.760
earnings, you would still not
feel like you're being seen.

00:38:08.970 --> 00:38:12.790
And so, I just think it's
important to know your audience.

00:38:12.790 --> 00:38:15.905
And for me, I'm very confident
in my ability to perform,

00:38:15.905 --> 00:38:17.440
you can check the receipts.

00:38:18.890 --> 00:38:23.685
But what I really want to make sure,
is that a Chelsea can have a shot and

00:38:23.685 --> 00:38:29.639
when there's more than just rather than I
leading a fortune 500 as 2 black women,

00:38:29.639 --> 00:38:33.790
then maybe I won't need to
answer those questions.

00:38:33.790 --> 00:38:37.910
And to be quite honest, if the question
wasn't asked, I would bring it up.

00:38:37.910 --> 00:38:42.180
Because I need to make sure that more
women, and more women of color, and

00:38:42.180 --> 00:38:47.870
more people of color, can understand that
their voice is necessary and required.

00:38:47.870 --> 00:38:52.360
And I cannot water down our history,
I cannot water down our story.

00:38:52.360 --> 00:38:57.153
And the more than I'm okay bringing
that narrative into the boardrooms and

00:38:57.153 --> 00:39:02.785
it's a corporate America, from my hair,
braid, straight to my changing of glasses,

00:39:02.785 --> 00:39:07.100
to raising a black boy,
all of those narratives are important.

00:39:07.100 --> 00:39:08.887
So I'm not going to water it down,

00:39:08.887 --> 00:39:13.140
and in fact, I'm going to make sure that
those stories continue to be stated.

00:39:13.140 --> 00:39:18.062
Now if you want to ask what
questions people don't ask,

00:39:18.062 --> 00:39:21.808
that I wish that they
would ask me more of.

00:39:22.880 --> 00:39:25.840
I would just say more about purpose.

00:39:25.840 --> 00:39:29.150
Because again,
my performance speaks for itself.

00:39:29.150 --> 00:39:32.560
You cannot be a leader and not perform.

00:39:32.560 --> 00:39:34.740
Period into story.

00:39:34.740 --> 00:39:38.541
But what I want people to
really ask me about, is my why.

00:39:38.541 --> 00:39:43.275
Why do I believe I am the one with
the privilege of having this seat.

00:39:43.275 --> 00:39:47.484
And therefore I get to share more
about purpose and impact, and

00:39:47.484 --> 00:39:50.285
my purpose is to inspire others.

00:39:50.285 --> 00:39:55.358
And if I can share more about who I
am unapologetically, then hopefully I

00:39:55.358 --> 00:40:01.013
can inspire more people, more women,
more under privileged or the voiceless,

00:40:01.013 --> 00:40:06.190
to see that they have a shot and
they have a shot unapologetically though.

00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:09.740
So hopefully that answers the question.

00:40:09.740 --> 00:40:12.540
&gt;&gt; Definitely does, and
a very, very powerful answer.

00:40:12.540 --> 00:40:14.587
Thank you so much, really appreciate it.

00:40:14.587 --> 00:40:16.080
&gt;&gt; Thank you, thank you.

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:17.110
Such a great question.

00:40:18.320 --> 00:40:19.270
&gt;&gt; Fantastic question.

00:40:19.270 --> 00:40:21.852
I'm so fortunate,
my classmates is incredible,

00:40:21.852 --> 00:40:25.220
as you've just seen in a small snippet,
lucky place to be in here.

00:40:26.240 --> 00:40:32.230
Tashanda, we have a lightning round to
close, I hope you're prepared for this.

00:40:32.230 --> 00:40:36.030
&gt;&gt; Yes, maybe not, but we'll see [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We'll see, [LAUGH] all right.

00:40:36.030 --> 00:40:39.820
I know you're a basketball family,
so we'll start very simple.

00:40:39.820 --> 00:40:40.700
Jordan or LeBron?

00:40:41.750 --> 00:40:42.425
&gt;&gt; LeBron.

00:40:42.425 --> 00:40:44.550
&gt;&gt; Okay, I like to decide.

00:40:44.550 --> 00:40:49.970
&gt;&gt; I have to, right, the speaker
bringing brilliance to the boardroom.

00:40:49.970 --> 00:40:53.460
I love LeBron, but
definitely love Jordan as well, so.

00:40:54.640 --> 00:40:59.560
&gt;&gt; Okay, Kim Mulkey's 40 and
0 Lady Bears from 2011 to 2012 or

00:40:59.560 --> 00:41:04.050
the men's national championship
team from this past year?

00:41:04.050 --> 00:41:07.110
&gt;&gt; [SOUND] This March Madness was crazy.

00:41:07.110 --> 00:41:08.471
&gt;&gt; It was great.

00:41:08.471 --> 00:41:13.965
&gt;&gt; Wow, both,
I mean I know I have to choose, but

00:41:13.965 --> 00:41:19.200
my gosh, both amazing runs, amazing runs.

00:41:19.200 --> 00:41:22.647
&gt;&gt; That's fair, I was very happy to
see Baylor beat Gonzaga personally,

00:41:22.647 --> 00:41:23.389
but-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:41:23.389 --> 00:41:24.727
&gt;&gt; For another day.

00:41:24.727 --> 00:41:26.065
&gt;&gt; Another day.

00:41:26.065 --> 00:41:31.280
&gt;&gt; Better album, Beyoncé's Dangerously
in Love or Jay Z's Watch the Throne?

00:41:32.660 --> 00:41:36.460
&gt;&gt; I'm all about to bee hive,
so Dangerously in Love.

00:41:36.460 --> 00:41:37.040
I love it.

00:41:38.220 --> 00:41:43.690
&gt;&gt; Okay, should we pay off student debt
now or invest in our retirement savings?

00:41:43.690 --> 00:41:50.544
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Both, both, work to pay down
your debt and always stay invested.

00:41:50.544 --> 00:41:54.360
&gt;&gt; Awesome, last one, and there's no
choice, we just need your answer.

00:41:54.360 --> 00:41:57.435
So there's a bunch of x consultants and
bankers and

00:41:57.435 --> 00:42:00.520
folks that love their
points listening right now.

00:42:01.670 --> 00:42:03.420
How should we spend those Chase points?

00:42:03.420 --> 00:42:05.180
What's the best way to do it?

00:42:05.180 --> 00:42:09.150
&gt;&gt; I mean, I'm all about travel, and
hopefully we'll be able to unlock that and

00:42:09.150 --> 00:42:12.350
get back traveling all around the world,
travel.

00:42:12.350 --> 00:42:15.760
&gt;&gt; Where is the first place
you're going after this all ends?

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:20.033
&gt;&gt; I mean I've traveled to Texas, so
I've had a little bit of travel, and

00:42:20.033 --> 00:42:21.540
I can't wait to go back.

00:42:21.540 --> 00:42:26.332
But most importantly, I can't wait to get
my kids on a plane to continue to see

00:42:26.332 --> 00:42:28.490
the world, all around the world.

00:42:28.490 --> 00:42:30.800
I just think it's really important.

00:42:30.800 --> 00:42:33.970
&gt;&gt; Great, Tashonda, again, thank you so
much for this conversation.

00:42:33.970 --> 00:42:35.289
It's been absolutely incredible.

00:42:35.289 --> 00:42:40.140
&gt;&gt; Well let me just close
by saying thank you.

00:42:40.140 --> 00:42:42.930
You have been a phenomenal interviewer,
you had me smiling.

00:42:42.930 --> 00:42:47.850
I'm just looking at you and looked at all
the bios, you all are quite impressive.

00:42:47.850 --> 00:42:51.870
The future is bright because you all
have made a lot of great choices.

00:42:51.870 --> 00:42:54.716
And clearly being part
of the Stanford family,

00:42:54.716 --> 00:42:59.700
I can't wait to see what you all do next
as a family, as a village if you will.

00:42:59.700 --> 00:43:04.076
But remember to always reach for the moon,
because even if you miss you'll be among

00:43:04.076 --> 00:43:06.530
the stars, and
your future is pretty bright.

00:43:06.530 --> 00:43:07.533
So thank you so much for the time.

00:43:07.533 --> 00:43:13.251
[MUSIC]

